@@ -27392,7 +27392,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadeshdr10tunnel"; translated in Estuary (#33001)
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#41401"
-msgid "At 3840x2160 and 50 or 60 Hz, HDMI 2.0 does not carry RGB at 10 bits, so the graphics driver sends HDR as YCbCr 4:2:0 at 10 bits (or RGB at 8 bits). With this on, an HDR10, HLG or HDR10+ video goes out as YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bits instead, inside the RGB 8-bit signal that Dolby Vision uses, with its HDR metadata as always: the colour of what Kodi draws over the video (subtitles, the on-screen display) keeps twice the detail, and the video is carried exactly. It is used only where the display says it takes 4:2:2, the driver can send it and nothing else has the output (Intel and AMD graphics); anything it cannot take goes out as before, and the log says why. It applies with the next video. Off: HDR always goes out as before."
+msgid "At 3840x2160 and 50 or 60 Hz, HDMI 2.0 does not carry RGB at 10 bits, so the graphics driver sends HDR as YCbCr 4:2:0 at 10 bits (or RGB at 8 bits). With this on, an HDR10, HLG or HDR10+ video goes out as YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bits instead, inside the RGB 8-bit signal that Dolby Vision uses, with its HDR metadata as always: the colour of what Hades draws over the video (subtitles, the on-screen display) keeps twice the detail, and the video is carried exactly. It is used only where the display says it takes 4:2:2, the driver can send it and nothing else has the output (Intel and AMD graphics); anything it cannot take goes out as before, and the log says why. It applies with the next video. Off: HDR always goes out as before."
 msgstr ""
 
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
@@ -27404,7 +27404,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 #. Hades: help of setting "audiooutput.hadesgpuhold"; translated in Estuary (#32951)
 msgctxt "#41851"
-msgid "For an NVIDIA graphics card that sends the sound to the TV or the receiver over HDMI. While Dolby TrueHD, DTS-HD or Dolby Digital Plus (E-AC-3) is sent as it is, without Kodi decoding it (passthrough), the clocks of the card are held at their highest, and let go when the sound stops or Kodi closes. Try it if the sound cuts out for a moment every few seconds, mostly on a laptop that is on its charger: the card changes speed and the audio loses a few samples (the kernel log then says 'dma error'). While it is on, the card draws more power and gets warmer, and a card that does not allow it is left as it was: kodi.log says what happened. It takes effect when the next sound starts. Off by default."
+msgid "For an NVIDIA graphics card that sends the sound to the TV or the receiver over HDMI. While Dolby TrueHD, DTS-HD or Dolby Digital Plus (E-AC-3) is sent as it is, without Hades decoding it (passthrough), the clocks of the card are held at their highest, and let go when the sound stops or Hades closes. Try it if the sound cuts out for a moment every few seconds, mostly on a laptop that is on its charger: the card changes speed and the audio loses a few samples (the kernel log then says 'dma error'). While it is on, the card draws more power and gets warmer, and a card that does not allow it is left as it was: kodi.log says what happened. It takes effect when the next sound starts. Off by default."
 msgstr ""
 
